 league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 CLE project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Quinshon Judkins</strong>'s rushing projection (12 carries, 46 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, CLE funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>7 targets for 58 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> (TE, 8 targets, 56 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 6-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"CLE","col":"#FF7A45","players":[{"name":"Deshaun Watson","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":64.78,"book":null,"over_th":53.01,"under_th":74.22,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"c